 [17592186533835 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533835 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533835 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533835 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "44d37873-44a7-53bb-92db-059de13aaea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69802b94-6643-58fc-ad81-08af38307602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533880 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533880 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533880 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533880 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533880 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "846433e8-6e1d-515f-aa50-49417beb1847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533900 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533900 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533900 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533900 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ef4eccd-b77f-5147-9424-093fafb1f473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533960 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f60f859d-bc35-5505-9e52-c51c8f4b4755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533961 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186533402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533961 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533961 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533961 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186533402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533961 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6d9b3c0-3abe-5e9b-971f-c0fb9407dba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-186161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{-1,000}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15f99678-56db-56d3-a971-804057202f02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533980 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the number $-1,000$ is a perfect cube.\n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-1,000},$ we express $-1,000$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1,000} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)\\cdot (-10)\\cdot (-10)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = \\\\ -10 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1071 "The additive inverse of $30$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c77dd09-2d15-50f1-838b-9b9dd6ed6be5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533988 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the additive inverse of the number $30$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,(30)=-30.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n30+(-30)=30-30=0\\,✓\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1071 "Classify the polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ according to its degree and the number of terms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "320d3413-9845-54f4-9740-6e67695ad26e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533990 #edb/ref 1074 "The polynomial $-\\frac{2t}{5}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $1,$ so it is a linear.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a linear monomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-187260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the ratio table below.\n\n| $2$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $18$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35308452-763c-5cea-932c-69c3b89da098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533991 #edb/ref 1074 "We start with the ratio $4:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the left-hand entry ($4$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry ($6$).\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n- To create a numerator of $2,$ we divide the numerator and denominator of our fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To create a denominator of $18,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator of the previous fraction by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\times 6}{3\\times 6} & =\\frac{12}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we fill in the ratio table:\n\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $18$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1071 "There were approximately $28,\\,000$ factory jobs in the city in $2020.$  Economic forecasts predict that this number will grow by $35%$ by the year $2030.$  Which of the following is closest to the projected number of factory jobs in $2030?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e32c9fa-0237-548e-8a15-6b3954405e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534011 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the projected number of jobs in $2030,$ we apply a $35%$ increase to the $2020$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $35%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+35%=135%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.35:$\n\n$$\n28,\\,000\\times 1.35=37,\\,800\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of factory jobs in $2030$ is $37,\\,800.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2c/2c4f090ff980be893416857fde0665bce955cee19f6a9dd7634039cbbcc641b6.png)\n\nAlex is making concrete using crushed stone and cement. The amount of cement he needs for a given amount of crushed stone is shown above. What is the ratio of crushed stone to cement in its simplest form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bad718eb-7fd4-52e5-a648-56b0a89dfa07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534056 #edb/ref 1074 "One of the points belonging to the graph is $(60,15).$ Let's write the ratio $60:15$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{60}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $15:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{60÷15}{15÷15}=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio of crushed stone to cement is $4:1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-359057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common factor of the terms $25z$ and $10z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5f9c923-65fd-50b8-8fc2-266d6f6aaf68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534064 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $25$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,25.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,5,\\,10.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $25$ and $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,5.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $5.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $25z$ and $10z$ contain $z.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $25z$ and $10z$ is $5z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c4b96cbe8ea09940b84c1d9d03fc55bdb82c61388bfb66d99cc7c6abd847a1fd.png)\n\nThe inequality corresponding to the highlighted region shown above is $x${{field-1}}\n$-3\\text{ or }x$\n{{field-2}}$1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4c36616-a95f-51f2-89f5-60b31bb5cb88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534083 #edb/ref 1074 "The red rays contain all values less than $-3,$ or greater than $1.$ In addition, \n\n- the closed circle at $-3$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-3,$ and\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x\\le -3$ or $x>1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534108 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e5d4b1f-71f8-513d-8ca3-3ea352241efb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534129 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534129 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "646a95eb-3d50-5de4-b371-82aed596236d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534129 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534154 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9d03a39-8b21-5275-aa96-f0cc96d800f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534154 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534043 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
